<template><div><section class="section-y bg-ink text-bone"><div class="wrap max-w-4xl"><p class="label-mono text-mint">Standards · 8 min read</p><h1 class="type-page-hero mt-4 font-display leading-[.96] tracking-[-.05em]">ASME B31G, without the mystery.</h1><p class="mt-5 text-[16px] text-bone/55">The remaining-strength equation is a screening tool, not a replacement for engineering judgement.</p></div></section><article class="section-y"><div class="wrap max-w-3xl space-y-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-ink/70"><p>B31G estimates the remaining pressure capacity of a corroded pipe by treating metal loss as a local defect. The inputs are simple: outside diameter, wall thickness, defect depth and axial length.</p><h2 class="pt-4 font-display text-[30px] text-ink">What Vomtek calculates</h2><p>For each anomaly, the engine computes the Folias factor, a remaining strength pressure and a safe operating pressure with a defined safety factor. The result is compared with MAOP and ranked.</p><div class="rounded-xl border border-ink/12 bg-bone p-5 font-mono text-[12px] leading-relaxed"><p>z = L² / (D · t)</p><p>M = √(1 + 0.6275z − 0.003375z²)</p><p>P′ = 2 · S · t / D · [(1 − 0.667 d/t) / (1 − 0.667(d/t)/M)]</p></div><h2 class="pt-4 font-display text-[30px] text-ink">Where teams go wrong</h2><p>Using depth alone, ignoring interaction between adjacent defects, and comparing runs without aligning distance. Vomtek stores inputs, method and reviewer so the calculation is auditable.</p><p class="text-[13px] text-ink/40">This article is educational, not a substitute for a qualified integrity assessment.</p></div></article></div></template>
